A VLA's storage is not given back when its scope ends, in either backend.

C99 6.2.4p6 ends an automatic object's lifetime with the block it was declared
in; a VLA is allocated by moving the stack pointer, so ending its lifetime
means moving it back, and nothing does that at the end of an ordinary block.
Step 15 taught the IR pipeline to do it on every path round a 'for', 'while' or
'do' - which is what stops a loop's frame growing without bound - and left the
general scope rule alone.

So this fixture fails at check 1 (two sibling blocks should reuse the address)
and check 2 (a loop built out of 'goto' has no loop statement to hang the save
and the restore off, and grows every round - the one shape here that can still
exhaust a stack). Check 3 already passes under -experimental, because the
enclosing 'for' reclaims what the 'goto' out of the inner loop left behind.

Unmute when roadmap step 17 lands: a save at the head of every block that
allocates and a restore on every edge leaving it, which means teaching 'break',
'continue', 'goto' and the end of a block which scope they are landing in. See
docs/ir-codegen-design.md sections 6.17 and 10.
